Program Purpose:
---------------

   The program creates a useable Character ArrayList. 

Compile/run:
-----------

    Compile and Run using the command "unit_test"


Acknowledgments:
---------------

    Had help from course staff through Piazza

Files:
-----

CharArrayList.h: Interface for a simple Character ArrayList Class.

CharArrayList.cpp: Implementation of a simple Character ArrayList Class.
    The default constructor for this class initializes an ArrayList to a size 
    of 0, and a capacity of 0. 

unit_tests.h: A unit testing file for the CharArrayList Class. 
    Runs in conjunction with the unit_test framework to allow for testing of 
    individual functions.

Makefile: File to build the program.


Data Structures:
---------------

    This main data structure of this lab is an ArrayList, which is a 
    expanding array. If the number of items is ever equal to the capacity, the
    array will automatically expand, allowing for an arbitrary number of 
    elements to be inserted into the ArrayList. An advantage that this data 
    structure gave was that it allows for elements to be easily added and 
    removed. An interesting algorithm was the expand function as it had to 
    dynamically create a new array and transfer the elements and then free 
    the memory reserved before. Also the operator overload was interesting 
    as it reprogrammed the equals sign to make deep copies when two instances
    are set equal to one another rather than the normal shallow copies it 
    makes.


Testing:
-------

    I created some functions in the unit_tests.h class that tested attributes
    of my ArrayList functions to make sure they were working properly. These
    attributes included whether each function fufills its purpose and whether
    a function returns the proper error message given invalid input


Time Spent:
-----------
    I spent about 5 hours on the homework.

Questions
---------
    1. 
        a. Insertion 
            Fastest: pushAtBack 100 times -- 890
            Middle: insertAt middle of list 100 times -- 1464622
            Slowest: pushAtFront 100 times  -- 2944173
        b. Removal 
            Fastest: popFromBack 100 times -- 644
            Middle: removeAt middle of list 100 times -- 1671564
            Slowest: popFromFront 100 times -- 3365402
        c. Access
            Fastest: call elementAt() for middle of list 100 times -- 486
            Middle: call last() 100 times -- 609
            Slowest: call first() 100 times -- 611

    2.  In the first two sections, Insertion and Removal, the front operations 
        are always the slowest, the middle ones are moderate speed, and back 
        are the fastest relative to each other. I believe this is because the 
        back operations don't need to shift any elements up or down, the middle
        operation needs to shift only some elements, and the front operations 
        need to shift all elements up or down. This accounts for why the 
        front operations take the longest and back operations take the
        shortest. we dont see this different in the last section access,
        because no shifting needs to take place and thus the times are much 
        closer between the operations here than in other sections.

    
